# Access Control

Use the most restrictive visibility that still satisfies the call site. Do not leave members at `internal` (the default) when a narrower level works.

## Visibility Order (most to least restrictive)

`private` → `fileprivate` → `package` → `internal` → `public` → `open`

## Rules

- **`private`**: default for members used only inside the enclosing type.
- **`fileprivate`**: sharing within a single source file (e.g. co-located test helpers, file-scoped collaborators).
- **`package`**: API shared with other targets in the same Swift package (including unit tests). Prefer this over `internal` + `@testable import`.
- **`internal`**: cross-file use within the app module when tests do not need access.
- **`public` / `open`**: only for library or framework API consumed outside the package.

When adding or changing a type, method, or property, start at `private` and widen only when required.

## Testing

- Mark types and members that tests need as `package`, then use a normal `import CSCSEmulator` in test files.
- Reserve `@testable import` for legacy code or cases where `package` is not viable; do not use it to avoid choosing an explicit access level.
- Prefer testing through `package` public surface or injected protocols rather than widening `internal` state.

## Examples

```swift
// ❌ BAD — internal detail exposed to all module code and tests via @testable
struct RandomCadenceGenerator<RNG: RandomNumberGenerator> {
    func nextCadence(after cadence: Cadence) -> Cadence { ... }
}

// test file
@testable import CSCSEmulator

// ✅ GOOD — explicit test-facing visibility without @testable
package struct RandomCadenceGenerator<RNG: RandomNumberGenerator> {
    package init(rng: RNG) { ... }

    package func nextCadence(after cadence: Cadence) -> Cadence { ... }
}

// test file
import CSCSEmulator
```

```swift
// ❌ BAD — public when only AppContainer needs it
public final class SimulationEngine { ... }

// ✅ GOOD — package for app + tests; private for tick implementation
package final class SimulationEngine {
    package init(...) { ... }

    package func start() { ... }

    private func tick() { ... }
}
```

See [unit-testing.mdc](.cursor/rules/unit-testing.mdc) for test structure; see [dependency-injection.mdc](.cursor/rules/dependency-injection.mdc) for testability via protocols.
